WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.660
Our office building now has a building manager.

2
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:10.886
Each morning, the badge office prints every worker a fresh badge, good for one day.

3
00:00:10.886 --> 00:00:13.216
Door readers check badges, not faces.

4
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:16.578
One Monday the manager makes the rule strict.

5
00:00:16.578 --> 00:00:17.905
No badge, no entry.

6
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:26.496
By ten o'clock, the cleaners, the plant service and the courier with the morning reports are all stuck on the pavement.

7
00:00:26.496 --> 00:00:28.485
Nobody had told them about badges.

8
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.440
Every door worked perfectly.

9
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.919
The people who got hurt were the ones nobody remembered.

10
00:00:33.919 --> 00:00:38.640
Hold on to that, because we are going to make this exact mistake on purpose.

11
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:41.771
You've met half of this already.

12
00:00:41.771 --> 00:00:45.660
With ordinary HTTPS, only the server proves who it is.

13
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.605
The shop shows its ID card.

14
00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:50.053
You, the customer, stay anonymous.

15
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:52.728
Between services, that isn't enough.

16
00:00:52.728 --> 00:00:56.079
The fraud check wants to know: who is calling me?

17
00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:59.088
Mutual TLS means both sides show an ID card.

18
00:00:59.088 --> 00:01:01.344
Each one learns who the other is.

19
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:03.674
Now the real building.

20
00:01:03.674 --> 00:01:08.333
Istio's control plane, a program called istiod, is the badge office.

21
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:13.813
Here are two Pods in the namespace hello-mesh: a small client, and hello-devops.

22
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:17.227
Each Pod has an Envoy sidecar next to the app.

23
00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:19.380
The sidecar is the door reader.

24
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.425
What name goes on the badge?

25
00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:26.638
It comes from the Pod's ServiceAccount, the identity Kubernetes gives a program.

26
00:01:26.638 --> 00:01:30.223
Istio writes it in a standard shape called a SPIFFE ID.

27
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:36.294
This one reads: in the cluster's trust domain, namespace hello-mesh, ServiceAccount client.

28
00:01:36.294 --> 00:01:42.863
Two Pods that share a ServiceAccount share a name, which is why the lab gives each Deployment its own.

29
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.925
How does the client get its badge?

30
00:01:45.925 --> 00:01:46.688
Four steps.

31
00:01:46.688 --> 00:01:53.208
A small Istio agent next to Envoy makes a private key, the secret half, and a signing request.

32
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:59.221
It sends the request to istiod, with the Pod's ServiceAccount token as proof of who it is.

33
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:04.560
istiod checks the token, and signs a certificate valid for 24 hours.

34
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:10.097
The agent hands key and certificate to Envoy, and renews them before they expire.

35
00:02:10.097 --> 00:02:15.361
Nobody rotates a certificate by hand, and a stolen badge is only good for a day.

36
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:18.343
Now the client calls hello-devops.

37
00:02:18.343 --> 00:02:20.151
The app makes a plain call.

38
00:02:20.151 --> 00:02:26.647
Its sidecar opens a mutual TLS connection, and the two door readers show each other their badges.

39
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:32.658
The receiving reader learns who is calling from the handshake itself, not from anything the app wrote.

40
00:02:32.658 --> 00:02:34.475
You can't fake it with a header.

41
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.644
But not every caller lives in the mesh.

42
00:02:37.644 --> 00:02:40.906
Here's a Pod in another namespace, called outside.

43
00:02:40.906 --> 00:02:42.928
It has no sidecar, so no badge.

44
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:45.200
Istio's default is PERMISSIVE.

45
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.301
Each door accepts badges, and plain text too.

46
00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.919
So the outside Pod's call still works.

47
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:52.713
Now our strict Monday.

48
00:02:52.713 --> 00:02:57.306
We apply a PeerAuthentication that says STRICT: mutual TLS only.

49
00:02:57.306 --> 00:03:00.465
What happens to the outside Pod's next call?

50
00:03:00.465 --> 00:03:01.613
Pause and guess.

51
00:03:04.833 --> 00:03:05.526
Refused.

52
00:03:05.526 --> 00:03:07.603
curl exits with code 56.

53
00:03:07.603 --> 00:03:12.277
The connection was cut before any HTTP answer arrived.

54
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:12.824
Well.

55
00:03:12.824 --> 00:03:18.001
That looks exactly like a network fault, which is why it's so confusing at work.

56
00:03:18.001 --> 00:03:23.955
The cleaner is on the pavement, and the door reader has no idea a cleaner was ever expected.

57
00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:30.482
So the rule: start PERMISSIVE, check every caller of the namespace, and only then switch to STRICT.

58
00:03:30.482 --> 00:03:33.097
Go strict after every caller has a badge.

59
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.452
Badges are verified now, so we can decide who may open which door.

60
00:03:38.452 --> 00:03:40.521
That's an AuthorizationPolicy.

61
00:03:40.521 --> 00:03:43.486
A workload with no policy lets everyone in.

62
00:03:43.486 --> 00:03:47.900
Say a second caller, a monitoring Pod, also visits hello-devops.

63
00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:49.848
Both calls work.

64
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:55.809
Now a team adds one friendly rule for hello-devops: ALLOW the client, for GET requests.

65
00:03:55.809 --> 00:03:58.236
They only meant to let the client in.

66
00:03:58.236 --> 00:04:00.533
What happens to the monitoring Pod?

67
00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:01.583
Pause and guess.

68
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.688
Locked out.

69
00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:13.923
Once any ALLOW policy selects a workload, every request no ALLOW rule matches gets 403, access denied.

70
00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:19.245
That one friendly rule became a guest list, and the monitoring Pod isn't on it.

71
00:04:19.245 --> 00:04:23.128
An ALLOW policy is also a deny for everyone it doesn't name.

72
00:04:23.933 --> 00:04:25.243
Two honest limits.

73
00:04:25.243 --> 00:04:31.718
In ambient mode, the guard on each node, ztunnel, sees connections, not paths or methods.

74
00:04:31.718 --> 00:04:37.465
A policy with HTTP rules has to sit on a waypoint, an Envoy for that namespace.

75
00:04:37.465 --> 00:04:42.776
If it lands on ztunnel instead, Istio fails safe and treats it as a DENY.

76
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:50.089
And Istio itself is one more big system to run, with its own objects, upgrades and certificate authority.

77
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.528
So the moral of the story.

78
00:04:52.528 --> 00:04:58.707
istiod prints every workload a one-day badge, and the door readers check it in the handshake.

79
00:04:58.707 --> 00:04:59.837
A badge says who.

80
00:04:59.837 --> 00:05:01.299
A policy says who may.

81
00:05:01.299 --> 00:05:05.020
And before you lock any door, list everyone who uses it.

82
00:05:05.633 --> 00:05:06.635
A quick check.

83
00:05:06.635 --> 00:05:14.362
You switch to STRICT, and a job in another namespace, with no sidecar, starts failing with a cut connection.

84
00:05:14.362 --> 00:05:14.649
Why?

85
00:05:14.649 --> 00:05:15.793
Pause and think.

86
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.285
It has no badge.

87
00:05:20.285 --> 00:05:25.584
STRICT accepts mutual TLS only, so its plain-text call is refused.

88
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.512
Next, lighter meshes, and the question this one leaves open.

89
00:05:29.512 --> 00:05:31.616
Does your team need a mesh at all?
